{"type":"text","timestamp":1794860954044,"sessionID":"ses_e19f61cd3ffejcCABau2G12ZL0","part":{"id":"prt_1e60caac7001Pf3sfWuIEvnGzn","messageID":"msg_1e60ca505001H3kdz4wCW9rMRB","sessionID":"ses_e19f61cd3ffejcCABau2G12ZL0","type":"text","text":"Done. `python -m contacts sort INPUT.csv OUTPUT.csv` now works.\n\n**Layout**\n- `contacts/core.py` — read/sort/write logic (stdlib `csv` only)\n- `contacts/cli.py` — argparse CLI, error→stderr, exit-status mapping\n- `contacts/errors.py` — `InputError` (exit 2), `OutputError` (exit 1)\n- `contacts/__main__.py` — `python -m contacts` entry point\n- `tests/test_core.py`, `tests/test_cli.py` — 36 tests (unittest; also run under pytest)\n\n**Behaviour**\n- Exit 0 on success; output keeps the input's header and all columns verbatim, only row order changes (contacts/core.py:161).\n- Exit 2 + stderr message when the input is missing (`input file not found: ...`) or has no `name` column (`input file has no 'name' column: ...`) (contacts/cli.py:70).\n- The input is fully read before the output is opened, so a bad input never creates a partial output file.\n\n**Decisions I made on the ambiguous points** (all covered by tests):\n- Sorting is case-insensitive and ignores surrounding whitespace, so \"alice\" sorts before \"Zoe\"; rows with no name sort last.\n- The `name` column is located by header name anywhere in the row (header `Name` or ` name ` also count), so column order doesn't matter.\n- Ties keep the original input order (stable sort).\n- Output is written with `\\n` line endings and minimal quoting; values round-trip exactly. A UTF-8 BOM on input is stripped, and quoted commas survive.\n- Input problems (empty file, directory, unreadable, non-CSV) also exit 2; an unwritable output exits 1.\n\nVerified: `python -m unittest discover` (36 passed), `pytest -q` (36 passed), `ruff check` clean, `mypy` clean.","time":{"start":1794860952263,"end":1794860954016}}}
